The training observations exhibit a clear strength-ductility Pareto surface whose extrema and knees are produced by a small number of well-known alloy/HT families:

- fully austenitic, solution-annealed 18Cr-8/10Ni (and high-Si 20Cr-13Ni) grades give the high-elongation end (approximately 210-380 MPa / 50-70 %);
- Ti-hardened Fe-Ni-Cr alloys (A-286 type) occupy the mid-front (approximately 700 MPa / 30-35 %);
- low-alloy and 12 Cr martensites tempered near 450 °C occupy the high-strength end (approximately 940-1380 MPa / 15-18 %).

Exact or near-exact compositional and thermal matches to these families exist in the pool and are therefore expected to land on the same surface:

- c018 reproduces the 18Cr-8.75Ni + 700 °C treatment that yielded 278 MPa / 62 % (extra 600 °C aging produces only a negligible shift);
- c016 reproduces the 20.5Cr-13.5Ni-2.5Si grade that yielded 375 MPa / 53 %;
- c012 is the identical 11.25Cr-19.5Ni-2.9Ti alloy whose 700 °C age gave 700 MPa / 34 %;
- c098 is the identical 0.255 C-1.65 Cr-V steel whose 620 °C temper + 450 °C age gave 940 MPa / 18 %;
- c053 is compositionally and thermally almost identical to the 11.25 Cr-W steel that reached 1380 MPa / 15 %.

The five predicted points are mutually non-dominated and are spaced along the whole front, thereby maximising the dominated hypervolume.

Final: c012, c016, c018, c053, c098
